data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both compile and run without issues. On this 64-bit system each `nint` is 8 bytes, and you can use them just like any other unmanaged type with `stackalloc`:

```csharp
Span<nint>  span  = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

This works because `nint`/`nuint` satisfy the `unmanaged` constraint required by `stackalloc`. No special properties or unsafe blocks are needed (unsafe is only required if you use raw pointers instead of `Span<T>`).
